---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";

import articleHero from "../../../../assets/beer-datamatrix-application-hero.png";
import LandingFooter from "../../../../components/LandingFooter.astro";
import LandingHeader from "../../../../components/LandingHeader.astro";
import { BEER_DATAMATRIX_APPLICATION_ARTICLE_EN } from "../../../../content/articles";
import { buildArticlePageGraph } from "../../../../lib/seo";
import { readPublicSiteConfig } from "../../../../lib/site-config";
import BaseLayout from "../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const article = BEER_DATAMATRIX_APPLICATION_ARTICLE_EN;
const config = readPublicSiteConfig({ PUBLIC_PHONE: import.meta.env.PUBLIC_PHONE });
---

<BaseLayout metadata={article} graph={buildArticlePageGraph(article)}>
  <LandingHeader page={article} phone={config.phone} />
  <main id="main" class="article-page">
    <header class="article-hero">
      <div class="container article-hero__inner">
        <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/en/">Markiro</a></li><li aria-current="page">{article.navigationLabel}</li></ol></nav>
        <div class="article-hero__layout">
          <div class="article-hero__copy">
            <p class="section-index">{article.eyebrow}</p>
            <h1>{article.heading}</h1>
            <p class="article-hero__lead">{article.introduction}</p>
            <div class="article-byline" aria-label="Publication details"><span>{article.authorName}</span><time datetime={article.publishedAt}>26 August 2026</time><span>{article.readingTimeMinutes} min read</span></div>
          </div>
          <figure class="article-hero__media" data-article-visual>
            <Picture src={articleHero} alt="A production specialist compares label, preprint, and direct-print samples for beer marking" widths={[480, 720, 1080]} formats={["avif", "webp"]} sizes="(max-width: 767px) 100vw, 45vw" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" data-article-hero-image />
            <figcaption>The application route should fit the pack, production run, and control model—not a catalogue headline.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <article class="container article-body">
      <aside class="article-summary" aria-labelledby="article-summary-title">
        <p class="section-index">Key takeaways</p><h2 id="article-summary-title">What determines the application method</h2>
        <ul>
          <li data-key-takeaway>For smaller batches, an adhesive label can be a complete starting method when the run is readable and waste is controlled.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>Preprinted packaging moves printing off the filling line but introduces coded-stock, lot, and spoilage controls.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>Direct print needs stable pack presentation, trigger synchronisation, code reading, and a defined reject path.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>Russian rules permit only one machine-readable identification means of the relevant format on a consumer pack or keg.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>One perfect sample is not acceptance evidence: test a run, restart, SKU changeover, moisture, and deliberate failures.</li>
          <li data-key-takeaway>Markiro controls applied codes, duplicates, case membership, and SSCC; the physical application system is accepted separately.</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>

      <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Article contents"><p class="section-index">Contents</p><ol><li><a href="#answer">Short answer</a></li><li><a href="#requirements">Placement rules</a></li><li><a href="#methods">Three methods</a></li><li><a href="#labels">Labels</a></li><li><a href="#preprint">Preprint</a></li><li><a href="#direct">Direct print</a></li><li><a href="#decision">Decision criteria</a></li><li><a href="#pilot">Acceptance pilot</a></li><li><a href="#markiro">Markiro boundary</a></li></ol></nav>

      <section id="answer" aria-labelledby="answer-title">
        <p class="section-index">01 / Answer</p><h2 id="answer-title">Which Data Matrix application method fits a beer line</h2>
        <p><strong>Labels usually provide the most flexible start for smaller or frequently changing runs; preprinted packaging can suit repeatable lots; direct print belongs on a stable flow after line trials.</strong> This is not a universal threshold based on bottles per hour. Pack geometry, SKU change frequency, available code area, reject ownership, and the ability to stop a suspect unit matter more.</p>
        <p>In every route, a unique code must be associated with one physical unit, read after application, protected against reuse, and represented in the correct application report. A supplier demonstration that shows only the print head or one attractive sample is incomplete if it does not account for unreadable, surplus, damaged, and duplicated codes.</p>
        <div class="article-callout"><strong>Follow the information, not only the mechanics.</strong><p>The complete route is: where the code is obtained, where it becomes physical, who confirms the read, how waste is recorded, and how the line recovers after a stop.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section id="requirements" aria-labelledby="requirements-title">
        <p class="section-index">02 / Requirements</p><h2 id="requirements-title">Where a code may be placed on a bottle or can</h2>
        <p><strong>Russian beer marking rules allow the identification means on the pack, closure, label, or sticker when it cannot be removed without damage and remains unobstructed for reading.</strong> For glass and PET bottles, official guidance lists the crown cap or plastic cap, neck label, back label, and main label. The code must not compromise mandatory consumer information.</p>
        <p>A consumer pack or keg cannot carry more than one identification means readable by equipment intended for Data Matrix and EAN-128. Directly applied symbols must achieve at least <strong>grade 1.5 (C)</strong>. The official beer guidance describes <a href="https://markirovka.ru/knowledge/tovarnye-gruppy/pivo-pivniye-napitki/kakim-dolzhen-byt-datamatrix-trebovaniya-k-preobrazovaniyu-i-kachestvu-naneseniya-pivo">Data Matrix conversion, placement, and print-quality requirements</a>.</p>
        <p>For an international equipment supplier, this means validation must use the Russian customer's final pack. Bottle curvature, metal glare, condensation, varnish, label seams, and embossed closures can turn a valid source image into an unstable physical symbol.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="methods" aria-labelledby="methods-title">
        <p class="section-index">03 / Comparison</p><h2 id="methods-title">How labels, preprinted packaging, and direct print differ</h2>
        <p><strong>The methods differ in when the pack is serialised and who owns quality control.</strong> A label carries a code printed locally and applied by hand or applicator. A preprint supplier serialises the label or packaging before delivery. Direct print personalises each unit within the production flow.</p>
        <figure class="article-visual" data-article-visual>
          <img src="/images/articles/beer-datamatrix-application-methods-en.svg" width="1200" height="620" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Comparison of label, preprinted packaging, and direct-print Data Matrix application for beer" data-article-diagram />
          <figcaption>Each route moves the principal risk: label placement, coded-material inventory, or print-to-pack synchronisation.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="labels" aria-labelledby="labels-title">
        <p class="section-index">04 / Label</p><h2 id="labels-title">When manual or automatic labelling is appropriate</h2>
        <p><strong>Labelling offers the most flexible entry point and the simplest product changeover.</strong> For a smaller run, an operator can print stickers on a thermal-transfer printer, apply them manually, and confirm each symbol with a 2D scanner. As volume grows, the same concept can move to an applicator or print-and-apply unit.</p>
        <p>Official guidance identifies manual labelling as suitable for smaller enterprises with limited production and describes applicators for higher speeds: <a href="https://markirovka.ru/knowledge/fast_start/marking_location/etiketirovanie-datamatrix-vneshniy-vid-oborudovanie-osobennosti-naneseniya">Data Matrix labelling methods and equipment</a>. Flexibility does not remove control requirements. The process must detect skew, folds, application to a wet surface, missed packs, and reuse of an already printed label.</p>
        <div class="article-cases">
          <article><h3>Strength</h3><p>Low entry complexity, short changeover, and a practical one-workstation pilot.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Constraint</h3><p>Manual throughput and placement quality depend on workstation design and operator instructions.</p></article>
          <article><h3>Control</h3><p>Read after application, record damaged labels, and prevent duplicate acceptance.</p></article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="preprint" aria-labelledby="preprint-title">
        <p class="section-index">05 / Preprint</p><h2 id="preprint-title">When pre-serialised labels or packaging make sense</h2>
        <p><strong>Preprinting removes the variable-code printer from the filling line but moves serialisation earlier in the supply chain.</strong> A qualified print provider receives codes through the protected service-provider route, prints variable Data Matrix symbols, validates them with machine vision, and returns coded material with a quality report.</p>
        <p>This route can suit stable lots and packaging already sourced in advance. Official guidance describes its use on metal cans, glass bottles, and PET packaging, and calls for re-verification after processes such as lamination or varnishing that can affect readability: <a href="https://markirovka.ru/knowledge/fast_start/typography/tipografskoe-nanesenie-datamatrix-preimushchestva-oborudovanie-protsess">the preprinted Data Matrix workflow</a>.</p>
        <p>The operational risk shifts to coded-material inventory. The producer needs a trustworthy record of the lot received, units spoiled before filling, residual stock after a product change, and protection against using a coded pack for the wrong production job. With many SKUs and short campaigns, this inventory can outweigh savings at the print stage.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="direct" aria-labelledby="direct-title">
        <p class="section-index">06 / Direct print</p><h2 id="direct-title">What an inline direct-print system must include</h2>
        <p><strong>Direct print becomes repeatable only when pack presentation is repeatable.</strong> Each unit must pass the print head at a controlled speed, orientation, and distance. A camera or scanner then confirms the symbol, and a reject mechanism or operator removes a failed unit before it reaches accepted production.</p>
        <p>Official guidance lists inkjet, thermal inkjet, thermal-transfer, and laser technologies, with three basic conditions: uniform movement, constant print distance, and at least grade C. It also gives possible locations for PET, glass, and metal cans: <a href="https://markirovka.ru/knowledge/fast_start/marking_location/praktika-pryamogo-naneseniya-data-matrix-vidy-upakovki-mesta-naneseniya-materialy">direct Data Matrix application practice</a>.</p>
        <p>Do not scope this route from print-head speed alone. The system includes product sensing, code queue, print trigger, inspection, association between read result and issued code, rejection, communication loss, and safe restart. A synchronisation error may produce a crisp symbol on the wrong unit: the camera reads it, yet traceability is incorrect.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="decision" aria-labelledby="decision-title">
        <p class="section-index">07 / Decision</p><h2 id="decision-title">Which criteria should be used in a supplier comparison</h2>
        <p><strong>Compare all methods against the same production case, not against the price of one device.</strong> Define a representative SKU and the hardest SKU, minimum and maximum speed, campaign length, changeover frequency, and acceptable manual intervention.</p>
        <div class="article-checklist">
          <div><h3>Product and pack</h3><ul><li>glass, PET, or metal can;</li><li>glare, curve, and moisture;</li><li>mandatory information area;</li><li>shelf life and handling.</li></ul></div>
          <div><h3>Production</h3><ul><li>volume and run length;</li><li>SKU and artwork changeover;</li><li>start, stop, and accumulation;</li><li>available reject path.</li></ul></div>
          <div><h3>Data control</h3><ul><li>code receipt and issue;</li><li>surplus and destruction;</li><li>read-to-job association;</li><li>report and exception journal.</li></ul></div>
        </div>
        <p>The total-cost model should add media, changeover labour, spoiled packaging, service, downtime, and residual coded stock. Our separate guide explains a <a href="/en/articles/beer-marking-cost-russia/">staged beer marking budget for Russia</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="pilot" aria-labelledby="pilot-title">
        <p class="section-index">08 / Pilot</p><h2 id="pilot-title">How to accept the application method before line-wide purchase</h2>
        <p><strong>Run real packaging and deliberately exercise failure paths.</strong> A carefully prepared sample does not reveal start-up units, condensation, label skew, roll change, missed triggers, duplicate pack presentation, or recovery after loss of communication.</p>
        <figure class="article-visual" data-article-visual>
          <img src="/images/articles/beer-datamatrix-application-pilot-en.svg" width="1200" height="620" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Acceptance pilot for Data Matrix application: packaging, production run, failures, and accountable output" data-article-diagram />
          <figcaption>Acceptance ends with reconciliation of accepted units, waste, unused codes, and the application report—not a read-rate headline.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <ol class="article-steps">
          <li><h3>Prepare the difficult pack</h3><p>Use the most demanding material, geometry, surface, and final label after all finishing operations.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Run a production series</h3><p>Measure stable rate, first units after start, stop and restart, SKU changeover, and media replacement.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Cause failures</h3><p>Test an unreadable symbol, duplicate, damaged label, lost network, and missing equipment acknowledgement.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Reconcile the balance</h3><p>Every issued code needs a clear state: accepted, rejected, unused, or subject to a controlled exception.</p></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section id="markiro" aria-labelledby="markiro-title">
        <p class="section-index">09 / Markiro</p><h2 id="markiro-title">Where the current Markiro boundary begins</h2>
        <p><strong>Markiro does not replace the choice of print technology, adhesive, print provider, or code location.</strong> The physical method and hardware compatibility are accepted at the facility. The current Markiro loop begins when an applied code reaches the workstation: format and GTIN are checked, duplicates are blocked, the outcome is journalled, and accepted items enter a case.</p>
        <p>The station supports handheld 2D scanners in keyboard or serial modes, retains permitted operations locally during unstable connectivity, and prints the case SSCC label through a compatible ZPL or TSPL printer. The current aggregation boundary is <strong>item → case → SSCC</strong>; pallet aggregation is a later stage.</p>
        <div class="article-callout article-callout--boundary"><strong>Application and production control are connected but distinct projects.</strong><p>Markiro can place the selected method inside a controlled workflow. Rated printer speed, physical print quality, and rejection performance for the specific line remain factory-acceptance responsibilities shared with the equipment supplier.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="article-conclusion" aria-labelledby="conclusion-title">
        <p class="section-index">Conclusion</p><h2 id="conclusion-title">Choose the route that accounts for every issued code</h2>
        <p>Labels, preprinting, and direct print can all be valid. The reliable choice is the one proven on the actual pack, with controlled waste and residual stock, repeatable restart, and an auditable journal.</p>
        <a class="button" href="/en/#demo" data-analytics="landing_demo_click" data-placement="article-conclusion">Review an application route <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
      </section>

      <section class="article-related" aria-labelledby="article-related-title">
        <p class="section-index">Related</p><h2 id="article-related-title">Continue planning the line</h2>
        <div class="seo-related__grid"><a href="/en/articles/beer-marking-line-equipment/"><span>Beer marking line equipment</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/en/articles/beer-data-matrix-not-scanning/"><span>Diagnose Data Matrix reading</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a href="/en/articles/beer-marking-cost-russia/"><span>Plan a staged launch budget</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
  <LandingFooter page={article} />
  <script>import { browserLandingRuntime, initLanding } from "../../../../scripts/site"; initLanding(document, browserLandingRuntime(window));</script>
</BaseLayout>
